<tr id="S3.T1.12.1.5" class="ltx_tr" style="--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">
<th id="S3.T1.12.1.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_th ltx_th_row ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">Sparse MAD</span></th>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">17.5<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium" style="font-size:78%;--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;"> (<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;" mathbackground="#F2F2F2" mathcolor="#D73C3C" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span>6.3)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">38.0<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:78%;--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;"> (<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;" mathbackground="#F2F2F2" mathcolor="#D73C3C" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span>14.9)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.5.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">38.3<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:78%;--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;"> (<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;" mathbackground="#F2F2F2" mathcolor="#D73C3C" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span>16.2)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.5.5" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">11.6<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:78%;--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;"> (<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\downarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#4169E1;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;" mathbackground="#F2F2F2" mathcolor="#4169E1" stretchy="false">↓</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\downarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span>1.2)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.5.6" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.6.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">20.3<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.6.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:78%;--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;"> (<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;" mathbackground="#F2F2F2" mathcolor="#D73C3C" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span>10.9)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.5.7" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_r"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">7.3<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.7.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium" style="font-size:78%;--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;"> (<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.7.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;" mathbackground="#F2F2F2" mathcolor="#D73C3C" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span>1.8)</span></span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.12.1.5.8" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb"><span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;">22.2<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.8.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium" style="font-size:78%;--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;"> (<span id="S3.T1.12.1.5.8.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="position:relative; bottom:1.0pt;"><math id="S3.T1.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\uparrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#D73C3C;--ltx-bg-color:#F2F2F2;" mathbackground="#F2F2F2" mathcolor="#D73C3C" stretchy="false">↑</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\uparrow</annotation></semantics></math></span>8.2)</span></span></td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</span></div>
</figure>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Experimental Setup</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Benchmarks.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Unlike previous studies that evaluate performance on full test sets, where modern LLMs often achieve high accuracy (<math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="&gt;80\%" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mn>80</mn><mo>%</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">&gt;80\%</annotation></semantics></math>), we focus strictly on the Challenging Interval. We constructed Challenging subsets for six benchmark:(1) <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Hallucination</span>: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">TruthfulQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">17</a>]</cite>;
(2) <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reasoning</span>: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">ARC-C</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">18</a>]</cite>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">BBH</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">19</a>]</cite>; (3) <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Domain Knowledge</span>: <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">LogiQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">20</a>]</cite>, <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MedQA</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">21</a>]</cite>, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">MMLU-Pro</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib22" title="" class="ltx_ref">22</a>]</cite>. These subsets are defined by instances where a naive single-agent baseline consistently fails, often due to logical traps or domain-specific misconceptions. For fairness in comparison,all baselines are evaluated on the same data subsets.More details are provided in Appendix <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Experimental Details ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Baselines.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate performance across varying agent group sizes <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{2,3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{2,3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math> using four distinct protocols. As a baseline, we employ <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Majority Voting</span>, representing ensemble aggregation without inter-agent communication. We compare this against <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard MAD (Decentralized)</span>, where agents engage in peer-to-peer argumentation, and <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Centralized MAD</span>, which organizes the debate under a board-meeting structure. Finally, we introduce <span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Sparse MAD</span>, a specialized debate variant explicitly designed for sparse signal recovery.For all the MAD method, we adopt agent
group sizes <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math> in our main comparison and will ablate on the effect of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math>. For single-agent baselines, we average across 5 independent runs.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Key Empirical Observations</h3>

<figure id="S3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2603.06801v1/ablation.svg" id="S3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/233;" width="476" height="233"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S3.F2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">Impact of Agent Scaling.<span id="S3.F2.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Performance on challenging subsets improves with group size (<math id="S3.F2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1\to 5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1\to 5</annotation></semantics></math>), indicating that multi-agent debate effectively filters correlated noise.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 When Does Debate Matter? ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> and Figure <a href="#S7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 7 Discussion ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> present the comprehensive results comparing <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Majority Voting</span> against three MAD methods across varying agent counts (<math id="S3.SS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N\in\{1,2,3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N\in\{1,2,3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>). Our analysis reveals three critical behavioral divergences.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">1. Voting Stagnates in Correlated Noise.</span>
As detailed in Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 When Does Debate Matter? ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, Majority Voting struggles to extract correct answers in the challenging interval. Across all datasets, the voting baseline shows negligible improvement, or even degradation, as the ensemble size increases. For instance, increasing <math id="S3.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>N</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N</annotation></semantics></math> from 2 to 5 fails to break the performance ceiling on tasks like TruthfulQA and LogiQA. This empirical plateau confirms that errors in these adversarial regimes are highly correlated; simply adding more static agents merely reinforces the false consensus rather than diluting it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">2. Debate Consistently Outperforms Voting.</span>
Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3 When Does Debate Matter? ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> demonstrates that this limitation is structural to voting, not the models. <span id="S3.SS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">All MAD variants consistently outperform Majority Voting</span> across the tested benchmarks. In the most challenging reasoning tasks such as ARC-C and BBH, debate mechanisms achieve substantial margins over the voting baseline. This indicates that the debate process itself, regardless of the specific topology, acts as a necessary “denoising” filter that disrupts the homogeneous error patterns of the crowd.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">3. Positive Scaling Dynamics.</span>
While “scaling laws” typically unfold over orders of magnitude, we observe a distinct <span id="S3.SS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">positive scaling trend</span> even within the limited range of <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=1</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S3.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mn>5</mn><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">5</annotation></semantics></math>. As illustrated in Figure <a href="#S7.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 7 Discussion ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, unlike the flat voting trajectory, the performance of Decentralized MAD improves monotonically with the number of agents.
Adding agents to a debate introduces new reasoning paths that help the system escape local optima, a property that is essential for breaking the Martingale Curse.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Debate is Essential in the Challenging Interval</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The empirical evidence decisively refutes the generalized claim that debate is unnecessary. While prior studies suggest voting suffices for simple tasks, our findings demonstrate that <span id="S3.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">debate is indispensable specifically in the challenging interval</span>. In regimes dominated by correlated noise where Majority Voting is trapped by false consensus, debate mechanisms such as centralized, decentralized, or sparse variants act as a critical filter to leverage argument heterogeneity and identify correct reasoning paths. However, although debate significantly outperforms voting, the absolute performance gains suggest that standard MAD alone is insufficient, motivating our proposed AceMAD framework to further amplify these sparse truth signals.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Theoretical Framework</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Having established that standard MAD under linear aggregation degenerates into a martingale in the challenging interval (Proposition <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem2" title="Proposition 2.2 (Martingale Property of Standard MAD). ‣ 2.3 Debate as a Martingale Process ‣ 2 Preliminaries and Problem Formulation ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>), we now present the theoretical foundation of <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AceMAD</span>. We formally demonstrate how our protocol breaks the Martingale Curse by (1) revealing asymmetric cognitive potential energy through enhanced observables, (2) statistically quantifying this potential energy via proper scoring, and (3) converting potential energy into submartingale drift toward truth through nonlinear amplification.
Detailed proofs for all theorems are provided in Appendix <a href="#A5" title="Appendix E Theoretical Analysis of AceMAD ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">E</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy in MAD</h3>

<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To formalize the cognitive asymmetry, we characterize the observable signals available in different debate protocols.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.1</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Debate Observable Space</span>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> be the space of natural language arguments and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\mathcal{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\mathcal{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> be the belief simplex. We define the observable transcript:</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Standard MAD</span> outputs a transcript consisting solely of arguments and self-reported beliefs:</p>
<table id="S4.E5" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\text{std}}=\{(m_{i}^{(t)},p_{i}^{(t)})\}_{i=1}^{N}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>std</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\text{std}}=\{(m_{i}^{(t)},p_{i}^{(t)})\}_{i=1}^{N}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The <span id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AceMAD</span> outputs an augmented transcript that includes peer prediction and realized scores:</p>
<table id="S4.E6" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{\text{info}}=\{(m_{i}^{(t)},p_{i}^{(t)},\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)},S_{i}^{(t)})\}_{i=1}^{N}," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Z</mi><mtext>info</mtext></msub><mo>=</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>N</mi></msubsup></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{\text{info}}=\{(m_{i}^{(t)},p_{i}^{(t)},\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)},S_{i}^{(t)})\}_{i=1}^{N},</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p3.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)}\in\Delta(\mathcal{Y})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)}\in\Delta(\mathcal{Y})</annotation></semantics></math> represents agent <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s prediction of the peer belief distribution, and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> is the realized score.</p>
</div>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Interpretation.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The key distinction lies in the <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">second-order beliefs</em> <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\hat{q}_{i}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\hat{q}_{i}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. Standard MAD observes only first-order beliefs (what agents think is true), while <span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AceMAD</span> additionally captures <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">meta-cognitive signals</em> (what agents think others believe). This distinction is not merely informational, it reveals <em id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">asymmetric cognitive potential energy</em>. Truth-holders who understand collective error possess fundamentally different second-order models than a hallucinating majority that assumes consensus.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.2</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Information-Theoretic Manifestation of Cognitive Potential</span>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{std}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{std}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{info}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{info}</annotation></semantics></math> be the information channels corresponding to standard MAD and AceMAD, respectively. There exists a stochastic kernel <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma_{std}=\kappa\circ\sigma_{info}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>κ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">∘</mo><msub><mi>σ</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{std}=\kappa\circ\sigma_{info}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consequently, <math id="S4.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\sigma_{info}\succeq_{Blackwell}\sigma_{std}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><mo>⪰</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>a</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>k</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>w</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>e</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>l</mi></mrow></msub><mi>σ</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma_{info}\succeq_{Blackwell}\sigma_{std}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Insight:</span> Information-theoretically, AceMAD provides a strictly richer signal space. Decision-makers observing <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{info}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>n</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>f</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>o</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{info}</annotation></semantics></math> can always reconstruct <math id="S4.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z_{std}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>Z</mi><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>t</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z_{std}</annotation></semantics></math> (by discarding forecasts), but the reverse is impossible. This extra signal is crucial for identifying truth-holders.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Quantifying Cognitive Potential via Proper Scoring</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the challenging interval, the majority is confidently wrong. To distinguish truth-holders, we must quantify the <em id="S4.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">potential energy gap</em> between agents who merely hold correct beliefs and those who understand why others error.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.3</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Peer Prediction Scoring</span>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem3.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The realized average belief of agent <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s peers is defined as:</p>
<table id="S4.E7" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bar{Q}_{-i}^{(t)}=\frac{1}{N-1}\sum_{j\neq i}p_{j}^{(t)}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>≠</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></munder><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bar{Q}_{-i}^{(t)}=\frac{1}{N-1}\sum_{j\neq i}p_{j}^{(t)}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We evaluate agent <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math>’s prediction <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> using the Brier Score function <math id="S4.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S:\Delta(\mathcal{Y})\times\Delta(\mathcal{Y})\to[0,1]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">×</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒴</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S:\Delta(\mathcal{Y})\times\Delta(\mathcal{Y})\to[0,1]</annotation></semantics></math>:</p>
<table id="S4.E8" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}^{(t)}=1-||\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)}-\bar{Q}_{-i}^{(t)}||_{2}^{2}." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>Q</mi><mo>¯</mo></mover><mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">‖</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}^{(t)}=1-||\hat{q}_{i}^{(t)}-\bar{Q}_{-i}^{(t)}||_{2}^{2}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.4</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Cognitive Potential Energy Separation</span>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider the challenging interval where the Crowd <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> holds a shared misconception and the truth-holder <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> holds the truth. Assume <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="E" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>E</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">E</annotation></semantics></math> possesses a correct second-order model of the crowd’s error. Then, according to the scoring rule in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem3" title="Definition 4.3 (Peer Prediction Scoring). ‣ 4.2 Quantifying Cognitive Potential via Proper Scoring ‣ 4 Theoretical Framework ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>, the expected score of the truth-holder is strictly greater than that of any crowd agent <math id="S4.Thmtheorem4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E9" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[S_{E}]&gt;\mathbb{E}[S_{c}]." display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[S_{E}]&gt;\mathbb{E}[S_{c}].</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Intuition:</span> The score separation stems from “surprise.” The Crowd, suffering from the False Consensus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite">[<a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">12</a>]</cite>, erroneously predicts that everyone agrees with them. When the truth-holder dissents, the Crowd incurs a prediction penalty. Conversely, the truth-holder anticipates the Crowd’s hallucination, minimizing surprise, and maximizing their score.This score differential is the quantitative signature of <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">asymmetric cognitive potential energy:</span> it reveals who possesses superior meta-cognitive understanding of the system’s epistemic state.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Converting Potential into Submartingale Drift</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The standard MAD uses linear averaging, which preserves the martingale property. To break this curse, we must release the latent cognitive potential energy identified by scoring, converting it into directed motion toward truth.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 4.5</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Nonlinear Potential-to-Influence Conversion</span>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math> be the influence weight of agent <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="i" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>i</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">i</annotation></semantics></math> at round <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="t" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>t</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">t</annotation></semantics></math>. We define the Multiplicative Weight Update rule as:</p>
<table id="S4.E10" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{i}^{(t+1)}=w_{i}^{(t)}\cdot\exp(\eta\cdot S_{i}^{(t)})," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi>exp</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msubsup><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{i}^{(t+1)}=w_{i}^{(t)}\cdot\exp(\eta\cdot S_{i}^{(t)}),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> is the amplification rate parameter. The aggregated belief is then <math id="S4.Thmtheorem5.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{w}^{(t)}\propto\sum_{i}w_{i}^{(t)}p_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo rspace="0.111em">∝</mo><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><mrow><msubsup><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{w}^{(t)}\propto\sum_{i}w_{i}^{(t)}p_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_theorem">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Theorem 4.6</span></span><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(<span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Submartingale Convergence: Breaking the Curse</span>)<span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu_{t}=p_{w}^{(t)}(y^{*})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>w</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>∗</mo></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu_{t}=p_{w}^{(t)}(y^{*})</annotation></semantics></math> be the probability assigned to the ground truth by the weighted aggregate belief. Under the conditions of Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Cognitive Potential Energy Separation). ‣ 4.2 Quantifying Cognitive Potential via Proper Scoring ‣ 4 Theoretical Framework ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> and using the update rule in Definition <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem5" title="Definition 4.5 (Nonlinear Potential-to-Influence Conversion). ‣ 4.3 Converting Potential into Submartingale Drift ‣ 4 Theoretical Framework ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.5</span></a>, for sufficiently small <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\eta&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\eta&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math>, the process <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{\mu_{t}\}_{t\geq 0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>≥</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{\mu_{t}\}_{t\geq 0}</annotation></semantics></math> forms a <span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">sub-martingale</span>:</span></p>
<table id="S4.E11" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_align_center"><math id="S4.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[\mu_{t+1}\mid H^{(t)}]\geq\mu_{t}+\Delta(\eta)," display="block" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0.167em">∣</mo><msup><mi>H</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≥</mo><mrow><msub><mi>μ</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[\mu_{t+1}\mid H^{(t)}]\geq\mu_{t}+\Delta(\eta),</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S4.Thmtheorem6.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Delta(\eta)&gt;0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">Δ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>η</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Delta(\eta)&gt;0</annotation></semantics></math> represents a positive drift toward the truth.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Breaking the Martingal Curse:</span> Unlike the random walk of standard MAD, it guarantees that the system’s belief in the truth monotonically increases in expectation.
The mechanism is clear: (1) Theorem <a href="#S4.Thmtheorem4" title="Theorem 4.4 (Cognitive Potential Energy Separation). ‣ 4.2 Quantifying Cognitive Potential via Proper Scoring ‣ 4 Theoretical Framework ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.4</span></a> ensures truth-holders consistently achieve higher scores due to their superior cognitive potential; (2) Exponential weight accumulation (<math id="S4.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="w_{E}\propto e^{\eta\sum S_{E}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>E</mi></msub><mo>∝</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>η</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mstyle scriptlevel="-1"><mrow><mo maxsize="0.700em" minsize="0.700em" stretchy="true">∑</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.700em">S</mi><mi mathsize="0.710em">E</mi></msub></mrow></mstyle></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">w_{E}\propto e^{\eta\sum S_{E}}</annotation></semantics></math>) amplifies this advantage over rounds; (3) Eventually, the truth-holder’s influence dominates the aggregate, pulling the system toward the correct answer even from a minority starting position.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>The AceMAD Protocol</h2>

<figure id="S5.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2603.06801v1/method.png" id="S5.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/309;" width="405" height="309" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S5.F3.8" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S5.F3.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold" style="font-size:90%;">The AceMAD: Converting Asymmetric Cognitive Potential into Submartingale Drift.<span id="S5.F3.9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> The process iterates through four phases: (1) </span>Argumentation:<span id="S5.F3.9.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Agents exchange arguments (<math id="S5.F3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="m_{i}^{(t)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mi>m</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">m_{i}^{(t)}</annotation></semantics></math>) in a shared context; (2) </span>Signal Extraction:<span id="S5.F3.9.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Agents privately commit to their self-belief (<math id="S5.F3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>) and peer-prediction (<math id="S5.F3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>q</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>),revealing asymmetric second-order cognition; (3) </span>Verification:<span id="S5.F3.9.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Brier Score <math id="S5.F3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="S_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">S_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> quantifies the cognitive potential gap; (4) </span>Non-linear Amplification:<span id="S5.F3.9.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> Exponential weight updates convert potential into directional drift, progressively amplifying truth-holders’ influence until they dominate the aggregate, breaking the Martingale Curse.</span></span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Based on the theoretical framework established in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Theoretical Framework ‣ Breaking the Martingale Curse: Multi-Agent Debate via Asymmetric Cognitive Potential Energy" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>,we present the concrete realization of asymmetric cognitive potential energy in the <span id="S5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">AceMAD</span>. The algorithm operationalizes our theory through a four-phase cycle that extracts, quantifies, and amplifies the latent cognitive asymmetry.</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">5.1 </span>Protocol Design</h3>

<figure id="S5.T2" class="ltx_table">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S5.T2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Table 2</span>: </span><span id="S5.T2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Main results on challenging subsets (<math id="S5.T2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=5" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=5</annotation></semantics></math>). We evaluate AceMAD (<math id="S5.T2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="T\in\{2,3,5\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">{</mo><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="1.110em" minsize="1.110em">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">T\in\{2,3,5\}</annotation></semantics></math>) against different baselines across six benchmarks using both GPT-4o-mini and open-source models. Performance gains over the best baseline are shown in parentheses.</span></figcaption>
<div id="S5.T2.5" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_center ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:345.0pt;height:129.8pt;vertical-align:-63.5pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="transform:translate(-132.6pt,49.9pt) scale(0.565463144589938,0.565463144589938) ;">
<table id="S5.T2.5.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_guessed_headers ltx_align_middle">
<thead class="ltx_thead">
<tr id="S5.T2.5.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
